Live endpoint evidence: POST /api/canvas-sync discovered, not probed (state-changing); GET /api/courses returned 200 in 51ms; GET /api/courses/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (needs a real id and none was available from a sibling list endpoint); GET /api/tasks returned 200 in 48ms; GET /api/tasks/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (needs a real id and none was available from a sibling list endpoint); POST /api/tasks discovered, not probed (state-changing); PUT /api/tasks/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (state-changing); DELETE /api/tasks/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (state-changing).

IMPLEMENTATION: Canvas resync is landing correctly against `ISharedCanvasClient`, but flagging a data-type concern per the context file's "overdue" filter (DueDate in the past and Status != Completed): `TaskEntity.DueDate`, `Course`/`Task` Canvas timestamp fields, and the corresponding DTOs are typed `DateTimeOffset`, and SQLite (via the EF Core Sqlite provider) has no native `DateTimeOffset` column type - it's stored as TEXT and comparisons across values with different embedded offsets (e.g. a locally-created task vs. one upserted from a Canvas assignment payload) are not guaranteed to sort/compare the way `DateTimeOffset`'s built-in UTC-normalized comparison would in memory. Given "overdue" filtering does exactly this kind of comparison against `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`, recommend standardizing on `DateTime` (stored as UTC) across `TaskEntity`, `Course`, and the Canvas DTOs before this ships, rather than mixing offsets through SQLite text comparisons.

REVIEW: Agree this is a legitimate, evidence-backed risk rather than a style preference - it's a documented EF Core Sqlite provider limitation, and the context file's own "overdue" filter definition depends on correct DateTime comparison. Recommend treating as a should-fix before merge, not just a follow-up ticket.

